/**
  @page examples-distance-normalization Constructing a distance approximation from a Poisson field
  @brief Mesh three implicit disks, solve a signed auxiliary problem and inspect its normalization error.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section examples-distance-setting The geometric reference

  Let @f$D=[0,1]^2@f$ contain three disks with centers and radii

  | Center | Radius |
  | :--- | ---: |
  | @f$(0.75,0.25)@f$ | @f$0.05@f$ |
  | @f$(0.25,0.25)@f$ | @f$0.25@f$ |
  | @f$(0.75,0.75)@f$ | @f$0.10@f$ |

  The largest disk touches the square at two points. The disks have disjoint
  interiors. Their union has signed distance
  @f[
    d(x)=\min_{i=1,2,3}\bigl(\lVert x-c_i\rVert-R_i\bigr),
  @f]
  negative inside and positive outside. This analytic field supplies both the
  implicit geometry and a reference for the normalized output.

  @snippet Models/Distance/SpaldingTucker.cpp distance-target

  @section examples-distance-mesh Resolve and label the interface

  The background has 32 vertices per axis. Its nodal @f$P_1@f$ interpolation
  approximates the signed-distance field. MMG cuts the zero level set of that
  interpolant and remeshes with maximum requested edge size @f$0.02@f$.

  @snippet Models/Distance/SpaldingTucker.cpp distance-remesh

  The material split explicitly assigns inside cells attribute 3, outside
  cells attribute 2 and interface edges attribute 5. The field used to drive
  remeshing is scoped to that operation. The following solve uses a new space
  on the returned mesh.

  In the checked run, the result has 3,852 vertices and 7,458 triangles. Its
  inside area is @f$0.2339284@f$, compared with the analytic disk area
  @f$\pi\sum_iR_i^2=0.2356194@f$. Refining the background interpolation is
  part of controlling this geometric discrepancy; remeshing a fixed
  interpolant retains its zero-set approximation.

  @section examples-distance-potential Solve the auxiliary problem

  With @f$\Omega_-@f$ the mesh's inside region and @f$\Gamma_h@f$ its marked
  interface, the scalar potential satisfies
  @f[
    -\Delta u=1-2\chi_{\Omega_-}\quad\text{in }D,
    \qquad u=0\quad\text{on }\Gamma_h.
  @f]
  The exterior boundary portions have the homogeneous Neumann condition of
  the weak formulation. The force is negative inside and positive outside.
  The fitted interface provides the essential condition that anchors each
  region. The corresponding constrained stiffness matrix is symmetric
  positive definite.

  The program constructs this problem explicitly so that CG convergence and
  the assembled residual can be checked before normalization. The same
  attributed forcing is used by @ref Rodin::Distance::SignedPoisson.

  @snippet Models/Distance/SpaldingTucker.cpp distance-potential

  @ref Rodin::Distance::SpaldingTucker evaluates
  @f[
    d_{ST}=\frac{2u}{\lVert\nabla u\rVert+
      \sqrt{\lVert\nabla u\rVert^2+2|u|}}
  @f]
  and interpolates it into the input space. A positive denominator preserves
  the sign of the potential pointwise. The expression requires a defined
  gradient and becomes undefined when the potential and its gradient vanish
  together. The executable checks the resulting nodal values for finiteness.

  @section examples-distance-results Inspect the approximation

  @m_class{rodin-mesh-stages}

  @parblock
  @image html DistanceNormalization-reference.png "Analytic nodal distance on the fitted mesh."
  @image html DistanceNormalization-normalized.png "Normalized signed Poisson field."
  @image html DistanceNormalization-error.png "Absolute difference of the two nodal fields."
  @endparblock

  The two signed fields use the same color range,
  @f$[-0.55,0.55]@f$. The error range is @f$[0,0.12]@f$.
  Dark contours show the meshed interface. Dense triangle edges are omitted
  to keep the scalar fields readable.

  The checked local run produced:

  | Quantity | Value |
  | :--- | ---: |
  | Maximum absolute nodal error | @f$0.110375@f$ |
  | Arithmetic mean absolute nodal error | @f$0.0191554@f$ |
  | Maximum normalized value on interface vertices | @f$0@f$ |
  | @f$\lVert Au-b\rVert_2/\max(1,\lVert b\rVert_2)@f$ | @f$1.13\times10^{-12}@f$ |

  The arithmetic mean is over mesh vertices and differs from an area-weighted
  error norm. The maximum occurs on the upper boundary near
  @f$(0.2000,1)@f$, where the normalized value is @f$0.391290@f$ and the
  reference value is @f$0.501665@f$.

  The normalization has an intrinsic multidimensional approximation error.
  For a single disk with the exact radial interior potential
  @f$u(r)=(r^2-R^2)/4@f$, the center value becomes
  @f$d_{ST}(0)=-R/\sqrt2@f$, whereas the signed distance is @f$-R@f$.
  For @f$R=0.25@f$, this gives an absolute center discrepancy of
  @f$0.0732233@f$ before any discretization. The three-disk computation also
  includes interface approximation, finite element gradients and the outer
  Neumann boundary. Distance accuracy is assessed through the reference
  comparison in addition to the linear residual.

  @section examples-distance-run Build and inspect the output

  Build the `SpaldingTucker` target and run it in a scratch directory. It
  generates its input mesh and writes `DistanceNormalization.xdmf` with its
  HDF5 companions. The arrays are `potential`, `normalized` and `reference`.
  `DistanceNormalization.csv` records mesh counts, inside area, nodal errors,
  interface values and the relative algebraic residual.

  The program requires CG success, a residual below @f$10^{-9}@f$, finite
  nodal fields, nonempty inside/outside regions and interface values within
  @f$10^{-12}@f$ of zero. The recorded distance errors describe this
  approximation and geometry.

  @section examples-distance-source Complete program

  The complete program is available in @ref Models/Distance/SpaldingTucker.cpp "SpaldingTucker.cpp".

  @section examples-distance-next Continue

  - @ref examples-eikonal-fmm — Arrival-time propagation and mesh refinement.
  - @ref examples-swift-background — Interface fitting driven by signed distance.
  - @ref guides-shape-optimization — Level-set transport and redistancing.

  @example Models/Distance/SpaldingTucker.cpp
*/
